body:prose = >`
  &ND v2

  # AEON Node Model Revision Candidate [# node-model-revision]

  This candidate defines the revised node contract for development. Adoption
  replaces the current node model within v1. Historical specification and
  conformance snapshots retain their original interpretation. Record the
  exact revision of the source contract, wire mapping and fixtures together
  when publishing the revised v1 contract and its implementations.

  ## Node declarations [# declarations]

  A node is one binding with an intrinsic tag and an ordered child collection.
  Its declaration begins with an angle bracket, then a nonempty bare or quoted
  tag, an optional structural identity, an optional attribute block, an optional
  datatype descriptor and optional parenthesised children. The closing angle
  bracket completes the node. An empty child collection may omit its parentheses.
  The children belong directly to the node. An explicit tuple among the children
  uses its own parentheses.

  In a document body, object or attribute block, the decoded node tag is the
  binding key. A node and an ordinary entry with that key in the same scope
  conflict. Attribute blocks supply their own named scope. Header recognition
  follows the header grammar. A quoted node tag such as [$ <"aeon:header">]
  declares an ordinary body binding.

  In a list, tuple or node child collection, the node occupies a regular
  positional slot. The index supplies its address. Repeated intrinsic tags
  are valid in indexed scopes. Container nesting counts a node as one level.
  Attributes and their traversal use the ordinary attribute-depth rules.

  An explicitly keyed assignment to a node literal, or an anonymous wrapper
  giving it external identity, attributes or type, is invalid in the revised
  grammar. Use the node declaration to carry these properties. A typed
  assignment to a reference remains an ordinary reference binding. Structural
  identities use the current alphabet and document-wide uniqueness rules.

  The node delimiter establishes its kind in strict mode. An optional inline
  datatype in strict mode shall use [$ node] with any admitted generic arguments.
  Other inline datatypes follow transport/custom admission. An inline
  [$ node<T>] declares a domain for the direct children. Retain Core descriptor
  preservation and profile-owned admission. An inline datatype denied by the
  strict node rule reports [$ INVALID_NODE_DATATYPE].

  ## Structural paths and reference availability [# structural-paths]

  An exact AEON reference path and an exact SANSA structural path over the
  corresponding portable AES document shall identify the same occurrence in
  the same scope. This applies to quoted keys, positional children and explicit
  attribute traversal. Top-level named nodes use their decoded tags in the path
  and indexed nodes use their positions.

  ~~~$ aeon
  <tag("Hello", "World")>
  first = ~tag[0]
  second = ~>tag[1]
  copy:node = ~tag
  ~~~$

  The clone and pointer target payloads are [$ $.tag\\[0\\]], [$ $.tag\\[1\\]]
  and [$ $.tag] respectively. The occurrence at [$ $.copy] is a symbolic clone
  reference. A reference written in a node's child collection takes one slot.
  Source write-back after whole-node materialisation requires a separate
  consumer decision.

  A node's attached attributes are available to its children under the same
  ordering rules as other bindings. References within its attribute block
  obey ordinary sibling availability, so earlier attributes can be targeted
  and a reference to a later sibling is rejected. Reference syntax admits
  only fixed structural paths.

  ## Portable AES node records [# portable-records]

  The development record uses [$ kind=NodeLiteral] and a required nonempty
  Unicode string [$ tag]. Use the tag's decoded spelling and the existing tag
  length policy. This field carries the intrinsic tag even for a positional
  node. A named node's tag equals the decoded terminal key in its record address.
  Node records carry identity and datatype metadata and use ordinary attribute
  descendant records. Emit attribute records before ordered child records.
  The complete profile validates direct indexed children and their ordinary
  positions beneath the node. A [$ NodeHead] record is invalid under the
  revised model.

  The candidate writes the tag as a distinct [$ tag] field in Telex v1 using
  ordinary payload escaping. Canonical Telex writes it immediately after
  [$ kind]. The Film v1 candidate encodes the tag as an ordinary string in the
  kind-dependent payload slot for a tagged node. Allocate a distinct unused
  Film kind code for that record and treat the former node and head codes as
  invalid under the revised mapping. Numeric allocation and exact golden
  bytes remain to be confirmed against the owning codec tables.

  Use the entire source node declaration as the node record's provenance
  span when source-backed. Preserve independent spans for attributes and
  children. Integrity and canonical byte mappings shall include the
  intrinsic tag. Verification of historical signed bytes uses the contract
  that produced them.

  ## SANSA and AEOS consumers [# consumers]

  The node is selected directly by its path. Its children are direct
  positional descendants and its attributes use the ordinary attribute-space
  transition. SANSA [$ &tag()] filters the current occurrence using its
  intrinsic node tag. [$ &key()] uses the incoming named key where the
  scope admits it. Indexed nodes expose a tag and position. A scoped root
  exposes its intrinsic tag and hides its incoming key.

  Remove [$ &headTag()] from selector grammar and head enumeration from
  the node property facet. Retain capability preflight, scope checks,
  metadata validation and per-inspection accounting over the revised
  occurrences. AEOS rule selectors use the same topology and intrinsic
  tag metadata. Existing consumer admission controls govern each surface.

  ## Canonical source and migration [# canonical-source]

  Canonical source emits an empty node as [$ <tag>] and preserves ordered
  children. Named nodes participate in normal reference-safe key ordering.
  Canonical spelling of a quoted tag uses the decoded tag and the existing
  quoting rules. The parser and canonical emitter preserve node identity,
  metadata and direct reference paths.

  A same-key collapse can keep its authored AEON reference paths. In portable
  AES and SANSA, the same index can select a different occurrence after
  cutover. Migrate different-key bindings, tag domain claims and separately
  meaningful metadata case by case. Keep revisions coordinated across producers
  and consumers because the v1 identifiers are shared by both revisions.
`
